import{t as e}from"./utils-COoNfFoj.js";var t={slug:`mariana-antaya`,influencerName:`Mariana Antaya`,influencerHandle:`@MarianaAntaya`,influencerAvatarUrl:`${e}/mariana-antaya-avatar.png`,heroDescription:`I wanted to turn my 2026 F1 predictions repository into a community platform where other ML builders can submit their own GitHub links, have their predictions automatically extracted and scored, and engage with one another through reactions, comments, and side-by-side model comparisons. I’m building it to reduce submission friction down to a single link while creating a recurring race-weekend cadence that drives engagement and helps teach my audience applied ML concepts—such as calibration and prediction confidence—through hands-on competition.`,assetTitle:`Paddock`,screenshotUrl:`${e}/mariana-antaya.png`,remixUrl:`https://paddock.pplx.app/`,promptTitle:`Try Mariana's prompt`,promptText:`Build a web app called “Paddock” — a community platform where Formula 1 fans who build ML models to predict race outcomes can submit their GitHub repositories, have their predictions automatically extracted and scored, and compare models with other builders. The core user flow should work as follows: users sign in with GitHub OAuth, paste a GitHub repository URL on the “Submit” page, and the app’s extractor agent reads the repository to pull out predictions for the upcoming F1 race. Each submission should appear on the “Upcoming Race” page as a card displaying the predictor’s name, model type, features used, predicted podium, and last commit time. Other users should be able to browse submissions, react using F1-themed emojis, leave comments, and compare models side-by-side. After the race concludes, results should automatically score predictions and update three primary leaderboards. Pull the race calendar from the FastF1 API, where the “Upcoming Race” is always the next race on the calendar. The submission window should open Monday at 00:00 UTC following the previous race and close at the qualifying start time for the upcoming race. Display a live countdown timer for the next race of the 2026 F1 season. Include the following leaderboard systems: Podium Accuracy, awarding 1 point per correctly predicted podium driver regardless of finishing position (maximum 3 points per race); Exact Order, awarding 3, 2, and 1 points respectively for correctly predicting P1, P2, and P3 in the exact finishing order; Underdog Caller, where points scale inversely to a driver’s championship standing so correctly predicting non-favorites is rewarded more heavily; and an optional Calibration leaderboard using Brier score calculations across the full probability distribution. Social features should include five reaction emojis on each submission (🏎️ 🏆 ⚡ 🚩 🤔), threaded comments per submission card, and predictor profile pages showing all submissions, current leaderboard rankings across all four scoring tracks, season-long accuracy statistics, and a timeline visualization of commit history to illustrate how each model evolved throughout the season. For each upcoming race, compute a “Wisdom of Crowds” consensus prediction by aggregating all submitted predictions. For point predictions, use the mode (most-predicted driver per finishing position), and for probabilistic predictions, use the average probability distribution. Display the consensus prediction alongside individual submissions and track its performance on the leaderboards as its own entity. Also generate a dynamic SVG badge for each predictor at /api/badge/{username}.svg that displays their current ranking, allowing predictors to embed a live badge directly into their GitHub README using a markdown snippet.`};export{t as default};
